{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And they were both naked, etc.</b> The reason for their having been previously free of the aspect of evil, and their totality being holiness, is that they resembled below the supernal faces above, for the aspect of evil that cleaves to man is what creates separation in the lower part of man, for there evil acquired its place. And this is your sign — the sign of the holy covenant, which the kelippah surrounds in the foreskin, and HaShem commanded to cut it away. You see that there a demoness found rest. And you will find that all the lusts of the wicked who commit adultery blaze in that place, and HaShem decreed to cover the repulsiveness. And you will find that our Sages of blessed memory (Niddah 13a) forbade one to place his hand, etc., and they said (Shabbat 108b): a hand to the thigh shall be cut off, for it kindles a strange fire and a fire not blown consumes him, and this did not exist previously. And we may examine the matter from what we see in Abraham our father, peace be upon him, who said (Genesis 24:2) to the servant: ״שִׂים נָא יָדְךָ״ etc. The reason, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Zohar), is that Abraham our father, peace be upon him, was not so much in equivalence with all created beings in this aspect, for he distanced the repulsiveness. And even though it was not distanced absolutely, and death proves this, nevertheless the repulsiveness was not to such a degree, and the holy covenant in his flesh was complete, it was clean. And according to this approach, the verse's precise wording is properly understood in its saying <b>and they were not ashamed</b> — for it should have said ״וְלֹא נִתְבַּיְּשׁוּ״ (and they were not embarrassed), intending to say that even though they were naked they were not embarrassed — meaning, the Torah declares about them that they had nothing of which to be embarrassed. And understand. And the verse informs who caused the aspect of ervah to separate in man's lower half, and it said ״וְהַנָּחָשׁ״ etc., and from this the consequences unfolded, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The citation ״(זהר)״ appears in parentheses in the source, indicating a Zohar reference; the specific location within the Zohar is not given by the author."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:17.547110701Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}